  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nCurrently, deep mid level/sfc low over far south central \nCanada, results in strong westerly flow across the Northern \nPlains. Associated wrap around moisture rotating through far \nnorthern North Dakota, with most cloud cover and showers north \nof the International Border area. Mostly sunny elsewhere with \nwinds tapering as you go farther to the south. Most observation \nsites within the Wind Advisory have seen below warning criteria \npeak winds, though there have been a few gusts to around 60 mph \nthe past few hours. Have 55 mph as a peak wind mentioned in the \nadvisory, and we've opted for no changes to headlines with the \nvast majority of sites in the 50-55 mph range for peak gusts. \n\nNear-critical fire weather conditions also continue this\nafternoon, especially west, where the gusty winds are combining\nwith percent RH values in the 20s. Conditions will quickly \nimprove after 00Z as the strong winds subside. \n\nMid level low continues east into Ontario tonight, with a mid\nlevel ridge rebuilding into the Rockies and a sfc high\ndeveloping into the Northern Plains for Tue. Similar daytime\ntemperatures tomorrow as we are having today, though with much\nless wind. Mid level wave lifting out of the Southern Rockies\nand into the Central Plains tonight, will bring increasing\nclouds across the Dakotas Tuesday. Majority of precipitation\nassociated with this wave is expected to remain to our south\nTuesday night.  \n\nFor the rest of this week and upcoming weekend, the CONUS\nweather pattern will have upper level ridging just east of the \nGreat Plains while a large area of upper level low pressure/long\nwave troughing develops west of the Rockies. Southwest flow\naloft results for the Northern Plains, with continued\nsoutherly mid level WAA into the Dakotas from the middle of \nthis week into the first half of next weekend. Temperatures peak\nFriday and Saturday, when daytime highs are forecast well into\nthe 80s to mid 90s. Will see moisture advection as well with sfc\ndewpoints increasing. However, with the heat and despite this\nincrease in Td's, minimum percent RH values will drop well into\nthe 20s to possibly mid teens Thu and Fri over west central and\nsouthwestern areas of ND. Right now winds are not expected to be\nthat strong, so fire weather concerns remain uncertain at this\ntime. \n\nNear daily chances for showers and thunderstorms under this \nflow pattern mid-week into the weekend. Currently it is looking \nlike our best chances for widespread precipitation will be later\non Wed through Wed night when diffluent flow aloft overspreads \nour region along with a frontal passage. More uncertainty \nthereafter.",
